  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain this morning, with clearing skies and \nbreezy winds from the north this afternoon.\n\nIn the wake of a cold front's recent passage across the lower \nMid-Atlantic, will now turn our attention to the increasing \ncoverage of rain across the Piedmont as an upper level trough \napproaches the central Appalachians from the west. Light to \noccasionally moderate rain will overspread the area before dawn,\nwith the greatest coverage occurring across the Piedmont, where\na few locations may receive in excess of a half inch. The \nremainder of our area will receive a quarter inch of rain, or \nless. After sunrise, rain will decrease from west to east, with \nthe last of the showers exiting the Southside, coupled with \ndecreasing cloud cover during mid afternoon. \n\nWinds will remain breezy from the north through the day, with \ngusts ranging from 20 mph for much of the lower Mid-Atlantic, to\nas high as 35 mph along the higher mountain ridges. Speeds will\ngradually diminish during this evening into tonight as high \npressure approaches, however latest data suggests that somewhat \nbreezy conditions may persist through dawn Sunday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Confidence remains high for areas of frost and \nfreezing temperatures across the mountains and foothills on \nSunday morning.\n\nThe passage of high pressure east along the Ohio River Valley \nearly Sunday will result in the coolest overnight temperatures \nso far this autumn season. With clear skies areawide, believe \ntemperatures across the mountains will fall into the upper 20s \nto the low 30s in the more sheltered river valleys. Confidence \nis high that areas of frost will develop elsewhere through the \nfoothills, even where temperatures will hold in the mid-30s. \nLatest data suggests that winds may remain a little too breezy \nacross the Piedmont for the widespread formation of frost, but \nmay see pockets of frost develop where winds can manage to \ndiminish to near-calm speeds for a few hours. The combination \nof frost and chilly temperatures tonight will introduce a strong\nprobability that sensitive vegetation will be damaged or killed\nby the cold. Efforts should be made today to protect sensitive \nvegetation be covering it or moving it indoors.\n\nLooking to Monday morning, temperatures will again range from \nthe upper 20s to the low 30s across the mountains and the \nfoothills, reintroducing the potential for cold and areas of \nfrost capable of damaging or killing sensitive vegetation. \n\nLatest data suggests that low pressure will linger off the \nCarolina coast through the early half of the coming workweek, \nbefore shifting east during the 2nd half of the week. Impacts \nfrom this system are most likely to be limited for our area, \nthough occasional rounds of shower activity may advance as far \nwest as the Highway 29 corridor.",
